{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And they shall come.</b> The word ונחו is milra, like כי שמו אותי בבור (Genesis 40:15) and שמו את שמי (Numbers 6:27)." "<b>The desolate places.</b> A place of desolation, from the root of ואשיתהו בתה (Isaiah 5:10)." "<b>And in the clefts of the rocks.</b> It has no cognate, and its meaning is according to its context." "Also <b>the thorns</b> are trees that have no fruit, and likewise ותחת הנעצוץ (Isaiah 55:13)." "Also the word <b>the thornbushes</b> has no cognate. Now they shall be so numerous that they encamp in settled places and places where there is no settlement."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The lemma cited is ובאו from Isaiah 7:19, but the grammatical point concerns ונחו from the same verse; the note explains that its accent pattern (milra, i.e., stress on the final syllable) matches the other cited forms."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:52:41.100790902Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}